 "footnotes": [
  {
   "id": "c-property-3",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "The book prints $\\langle n|\\krhohat^2|m\\rangle \\leq \\langle n|\\krhohat|m\\rangle$ for any states $|m\\rangle$ and $|n\\rangle$. The inequality holds for the diagonal elements, $\\langle n|\\krhohat^2|n\\rangle \\leq \\langle n|\\krhohat|n\\rangle$ for any state $|n\\rangle$, since the eigenvalues of $\\krhohat$ lie between 0 and 1; off-diagonal elements are complex and are not ordered. Equality for every $|n\\rangle$ means $\\krhohat^2 = \\krhohat$, which holds only for a pure state."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-eq-3-16",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "The book prints $\\sum_n\\krhonn$, which is 1 by property (2). The average photon number is $\\knbar_j = \\sum_n n\\,\\krhonn$, which with Eq. (3.14) gives $1/(e^{\\hbar\\knu_j/k_B \\kT} - 1)$."
  }
 ],
